    {
      "id": "ART-02",
      "chapter": "inputs",
      "title": "The ordinary class quotient parametrizes characters with the selected decomposition conditions",
      "anchor": "inputs-artin-characters",
      "statement": "For N≥1, precomposition with α_K identifies additive characters of Cl(𝒪_K)/⟨[𝔭]:𝔭|N⟩ into M with locally constant G_K-characters into M killing all finite inertia and all decomposition groups at primes above N. If K is normal over the base, the identification transports a prescribed conjugation action ρ on coefficients.",
      "hypotheses": "K a number field, M an additive module over a commutative ring, and K totally complex or M[2]=0. The converse uses all of G_K. The equivariance statement uses a common algebraic closure, a normal extension K/F and a representation of G_F on M.",
      "definitions": [
        "A character θ on the multiplicative class group is additive on products. The split-prime condition kills the prime classes individually.",
        "A locally constant Galois character has open kernel. Its fixed field is finite abelian, with finite inertia and real conjugation trivial under the stated hypotheses.",
        "Under conjugation by g lifting τ one has α_K(gug⁻¹)=τ_*α_K(u). The scalar action for the degree-one coefficient is χ_cyc(g)^(−e)."
      ],
